{% when 'attribution-identity' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">ATTRIBUTION & IDENTITY</span><h1>Know which marketing efforts lead to sales.</h1><p>Your advertising platform tells you someone clicked. Your store tells you someone purchased. Here's how TraceKit helps connect those two parts of the story—and why that matters.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>5 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / The problem with disconnected reports</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Follow a sale from click to checkout</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / How TraceKit connects the dots</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / When something doesn't match</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / What this means for your business</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why don't my advertising and store reports agree?</h2><p>Imagine your advertising dashboard reports 100 sales, but your store shows 93 orders. Which number should you trust? Each platform is looking at a different part of the customer experience. One records ad activity and attributed conversions; the other records actual orders. Comparing their totals alone doesn't explain the difference.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>The big idea</strong><p>Instead of choosing which dashboard to believe, look at the records behind each number.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Follow a sale from the first click.</h2><p>Suppose someone clicks an ad, visits your store, and buys a product for $129. Your advertising system records the click, while your store records the purchase. When the systems share a reliable reference, TraceKit can help show how those events belong together.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Customer clicks an ad then Customer buys a product then TraceKit connects the records"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Customer clicks an ad</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Customer buys a product</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>TraceKit connects the records</strong></div></div></div><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Illustrative example</strong><p>One customer clicks an ad at 9:11 AM and places a $129 order at 9:18 AM. A shared tracking reference—not simply the seven-minute gap—provides evidence connecting the events.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>How does TraceKit know which records belong together?</h2><p>When you click an ad or place an order, the systems involved can assign reference numbers. Think of these like tracking numbers for a package: they help distinguish one event from another. TraceKit uses available matching references to connect the advertising record with the correct order.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Find the tracking reference then Match it to the order then Show the supported connection"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Find the tracking reference</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Match it to the order</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Show the supported connection</strong></div></div></div><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Behind the scenes</strong><p>These references may include click IDs, network transaction IDs, and merchant order IDs. TraceKit keeps their original values and treats the marketing source and the commercial purchase as distinct records.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What happens when the information doesn't match?</h2><p>Sometimes a customer purchases without an identifiable ad click, or a platform reports a conversion that can't be matched to a store order. TraceKit's approach is to keep the available records and flag the missing connection for review—not guess or delete the sale.</p><blockquote>Missing information should be visible, not quietly filled in.</blockquote><p>This is where the technical term <strong>unresolved</strong> comes in. It means the available evidence isn't enough to confirm the connection. It's an exception worth investigating, not the normal end of every journey.</p></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What does this help your business understand?</h2><p>When marketing and sales records can be connected, your team can investigate which campaigns and affiliates are associated with actual purchases, where reporting gaps exist, and what happened after checkout. That can help you ask better questions before making budget or partner decisions.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>One important distinction</strong><p>A verified attribution link shows where a sale was recorded as coming from. It doesn't prove the ad alone caused the sale. Measuring that requires additional evidence.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>See the complete customer story.</h2><p>Explore how purchases, upsells, refunds, and other events fit together in an illustrative customer journey.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Explore Customer Journeys →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Explore Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'customer-journeys-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">CUSTOMER JOURNEYS</span><h1>See what happens after the first sale.</h1><p>A customer's story doesn't end at checkout. Learn how purchases, repeat orders, refunds, and disputes fit together.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>5 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Why isn't the first order the whole story?</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Follow a customer's purchases</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / What happens when money comes back?</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / What about subscriptions and chargebacks?</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / What can your team learn?</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article"><section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why isn't the first order the whole story?</h2><p>Imagine a customer buys a product today, adds another item at checkout, and makes a repeat purchase next month. If you only look at the first order, you miss much of what happened. Different systems may record each event separately, making the full story difficult to see.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>The big idea</strong><p>The first conversion is a starting point, not the entire customer relationship.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Follow a customer's purchases</h2><p>A fictional shopper clicks an ad, buys a $129 product, and accepts a $49 upsell. Both purchases matter, but they are separate orders. Where reliable references are available, TraceKit can help connect them without combining them into a single invented transaction.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Customer clicks an ad then First purchase: $129 then Additional purchase: $49"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Customer clicks an ad</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>First purchase: $129</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Additional purchase: $49</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What happens when money comes back?</h2><p>Now imagine the shopper receives a $35 partial refund. The original $129 sale and $49 upsell still happened. The refund is another event that changes the financial outcome. The example has $178 in purchases and $143 remaining after the refund, before other expenses.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>$129 + $49 − $35 = $143 before other costs. This is not profit.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What about subscriptions and chargebacks?</h2><p>Some businesses have recurring charges, canceled subscriptions, payment disputes, or chargebacks. These should appear as their own events, with their own dates and statuses. A pending chargeback should not be treated as a final financial loss.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Keep each event visible so you can understand what changed and when.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What can your team learn?</h2><p>A connected journey can help you investigate which campaigns are associated with repeat purchases, how much activity happens after checkout, and which orders later refund. When a link is missing, it should be flagged instead of guessed.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A trustworthy customer story shows both known connections and missing evidence.</p></div></section><div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how TraceKit brings business evidence together across the customer journey.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'data-reconciliation' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">DATA RECONCILIATION</span><h1>Why don't my reports show the same numbers?</h1><p>Your affiliate platform, online store, and payment provider can disagree. Follow a practical example to understand what's different—and what to investigate next.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>8 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Why do three dashboards show three totals?</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Start with an apples-to-apples comparison</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Walk through a realistic mismatch</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / What do we do with the four unmatched conversions?</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Why exact references matter</a><a href="#chapter-6">06 / Other reasons numbers can change</a><a href="#chapter-7">07 / A checklist your team can use</a><a href="#chapter-8">08 / What does TraceKit help you understand?</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why do three dashboards show three totals?</h2><p>Imagine your affiliate network shows 100 conversions, your online store shows 96 orders, and your payment processor shows 94 successful charges. It can feel as though someone has lost six sales. But the systems may be measuring different things: an attributed conversion, an order placed, and a payment successfully captured. Until we compare the underlying records, none of those totals tells the whole story.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>THE BIG IDEA</strong><p>A conversion, an order, and a successful charge are different events.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Start with an apples-to-apples comparison</h2><p>Before searching for missing sales, check that the reports cover the same dates and time zone. Confirm whether each includes test orders, canceled orders, upsells, subscription rebills, refunds, or pending payments. Check whether amounts use the same currency and whether a report counts orders or individual items. A midnight cutoff or a different definition of “sale” can create a discrepancy even when records are accurate.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Same reporting period then Same definition of a sale then Compare individual records"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Same reporting period</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Same definition of a sale</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Compare individual records</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Walk through a realistic mismatch</h2><p>Here is one fictional investigation. The affiliate network lists 100 conversion records. The store lists 96 order records. We discover that four network conversions have no confirmed matching store order. Among the 96 store orders, 94 have successful captured payments and two are still awaiting payment. This explains how the three reported totals can coexist—but it does not yet prove why those four network records have no matching order.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="100 network conversions then 96 store orders then 94 captured payments"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>100 network conversions</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>96 store orders</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>94 captured payments</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What do we do with the four unmatched conversions?</h2><p>We keep those four conversion records rather than deleting them or automatically labeling them fraudulent. We review their original transaction references, status changes, reporting times, and available merchant order IDs. Perhaps a conversion was duplicated, delayed, or never completed as an order. Until the evidence confirms the reason, the records remain unmatched and available for investigation.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>Four unmatched conversions are an open question—not four confirmed lost sales.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why exact references matter</h2><p>A $129 conversion and a $129 order that happened a few minutes apart might be related, but that is not enough to prove they belong together. A shared click reference, provider transaction ID, merchant order ID, or other known relationship is stronger evidence. TraceKit's approach is to preserve those original references and keep the marketing attribution separate from the commercial transaction.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>Matching references provide stronger evidence than similar amounts or timestamps.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-6"><span class="tk-guide-index">06 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Other reasons numbers can change</h2><p>Reports may also disagree because a customer received a refund, an upsell was counted as a second conversion, a subscription renewed, a payment failed, or an affiliate platform received a reversal later. Some integrations deliver events more than once or after a delay. Those cases should be examined as distinct source events rather than silently merged into the original purchase.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>Different event types and statuses should remain visible.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-7"><span class="tk-guide-index">07 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>A checklist your team can use</h2><p>When two reports disagree, start by recording the exact totals and the reporting period. Then check definitions, statuses, currencies, and source freshness. Compare the individual IDs and identify which records match, which have different statuses, and which remain unmatched. Document the explanation for each group and revisit open exceptions when more evidence arrives.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Define the comparison then Match known records then Review remaining exceptions"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Define the comparison</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Match known records</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Review remaining exceptions</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-8"><span class="tk-guide-index">08 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What does TraceKit help you understand?</h2><p>The goal is not to force every platform to display the same number. It is to explain why the numbers differ using available evidence. That can help teams investigate affiliate payouts, checkout problems, payment issues, and campaign reporting with greater confidence. Exact capabilities depend on the integrations and records available for your account.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>A trustworthy report explains the differences instead of hiding them.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how TraceKit connects source evidence across the customer journey.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'profit-financial-outcomes' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">PROFIT & FINANCIAL OUTCOMES</span><h1>Are your sales actually making money?</h1><p>Sales tell you how much customers bought. Let's follow refunds, advertising, product costs, and payment fees to see what the business may actually keep.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>8 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Why impressive sales can hide weak margins</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Start with a simple financial picture</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Start with Product COGS: what did the products cost?</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Why Product COGS changes which campaigns are profitable</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / How refunds and chargebacks change the story</a><a href="#chapter-6">06 / Account for COGS on bundles, upsells, and repeat purchases</a><a href="#chapter-7">07 / What if some costs aren't connected yet?</a><a href="#chapter-8">08 / Five questions worth asking about profitability</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why impressive sales can hide weak margins</h2><p>Imagine your store reports $50,000 in sales this month. That tells you what customers ordered, but not what you kept. Some orders may be refunded, advertising may be expensive, and every shipment has a cost. A business can grow sales while earning less on each order. To understand the outcome, you need to follow both the money coming in and the money going out.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>THE BIG IDEA</strong><p>Revenue growth doesn't automatically mean profit growth.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Start with a simple financial picture</h2><p>We'll use a fictional ecommerce business. It records $50,000 in purchases and issues $3,000 in refunds. For this simplified example, assume those sales were paid and no other payment adjustments occurred. That leaves $47,000 in collected revenue before expenses. Real reporting must account for payment statuses and settlement differences.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Purchases: $50,000 then Refunds: −$3,000 then After refunds: $47,000"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Purchases: $50,000</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Refunds: −$3,000</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>After refunds: $47,000</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Subtract the costs you can actually verify</h2><p>Suppose the business has $12,000 in Product COGS (the cost of the actual products sold), $15,000 in advertising spend, $4,000 in fulfillment and shipping, and $1,500 in payment processing fees. Product COGS should be calculated from the products and quantities sold, with a clearly defined treatment for refunds, returns, and recovered inventory. Subtracting those costs from $47,000 leaves $14,500. This is a contribution-style remainder after the listed expenses, not verified net profit. Salaries, software, taxes, overhead, and other costs may still apply.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>$47,000 revenue − $12,000 Product COGS − $15,000 advertising − $4,000 fulfillment − $1,500 processing = $14,500.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why a profitable-looking campaign can disappoint</h2><p>Consider two fictional campaigns. Campaign A generates $10,000 in sales from $3,000 in ad spend. Campaign B generates $8,000 from $2,000 in ad spend. Looking only at sales versus advertising cost, both seem attractive. But if Campaign A sells products with higher COGS, has more refunds, or incurs expensive fulfillment, its remaining margin could be lower. You need the downstream financial events and relevant costs to compare them fairly.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>A campaign with higher revenue isn't necessarily the more profitable campaign.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>How refunds and chargebacks change the story</h2><p>A refund reduces what the business keeps from a purchase. A chargeback may create a disputed payment, fees, and potentially a final loss, depending on the outcome. Keep the original order, refund, and dispute as separate events. Don't treat a pending dispute as an automatic loss or count the same refunded amount twice.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Sale recorded then Adjustment recorded then Financial outcome reviewed"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Sale recorded</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Adjustment recorded</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Financial outcome reviewed</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-6"><span class="tk-guide-index">06 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Don't overlook repeat purchases and upsells</h2><p>A customer may buy a product for $59, accept a $25 upsell, and return for another $59 purchase. Those additional transactions can increase revenue without requiring another initial acquisition click. Each product and quantity also has its own COGS, and those orders can introduce more fulfillment costs, refunds, or processing fees. A bundle or upsell with a different product mix can have a very different margin from the original offer. Looking at the complete customer journey gives a more useful picture than examining only the first checkout.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>First purchase + upsell + rebill can change customer value—but each event and cost remains distinct.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-7"><span class="tk-guide-index">07 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What if some costs aren't connected yet?</h2><p>Maybe your advertising spend is available but SKU-level Product COGS or product quantities are missing, or a payment provider hasn't supplied final dispute outcomes. You can still report the numbers you know, as long as you clearly state what is excluded. Avoid filling gaps with invented estimates or labeling an incomplete figure net profit. TraceKit's available calculations depend on the integrations, source records, and cost coverage configured for your account.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>Known amounts should be separated from missing or unverified costs.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-8"><span class="tk-guide-index">08 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Five questions worth asking about profitability</h2><p>Start with practical questions: Which campaigns are associated with refunded purchases? How much revenue arrives after the initial sale? What is the Product COGS for each product, bundle, and upsell? What do processing and fulfillment costs do to the margin? Which chargebacks have final outcomes? Which expenses are missing from our reporting? Each question helps reveal whether a headline revenue figure reflects a sustainable business outcome.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY</strong><p>The most useful financial number is one you can explain and verify.</p></div></section>
<div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how customer journeys, refunds, and attribution evidence relate to financial outcomes.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'tracekit-intelligence-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">TRACEKIT INTELLIGENCE</span><h1>What if you could ask questions about your business data?</h1><p>Learn how evidence-backed Intelligence can help you investigate the story behind a number—not just display another dashboard.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>5 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / The problem with endless dashboards</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Ask a question that matters</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / What should an answer show?</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / Why shouldn't AI simply fill in the blanks?</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Do you need a separate AI tool?</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article"><section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>The problem with endless dashboards</h2><p>You might have one dashboard for ads, another for orders, and another for refunds. When something looks wrong, you still have to investigate across multiple systems. TraceKit Intelligence is designed around business questions and the records that may help answer them.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>The big idea</strong><p>Instead of opening five reports, start with the question you need answered.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Ask a question that matters</h2><p>For example: Which affiliates are associated with the chargebacks we received last month? To investigate, you need dispute records, transaction references, and available attribution information—not just a total chargeback count.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Ask the question then Review linked records then Inspect the evidence"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Ask the question</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Review linked records</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Inspect the evidence</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What should an answer show?</h2><p>Imagine a fictional set of 12 disputes. Seven have evidence linking them to Affiliate A, three to Affiliate B, and two have no confirmed attribution. A responsible explanation shows the ten supported links and identifies the other two as unknown.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>7 linked to A + 3 linked to B + 2 without confirmed attribution = 12.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why shouldn't AI simply fill in the blanks?</h2><p>A convincing answer isn't necessarily a correct answer. When source records are missing or relationships are uncertain, TraceKit's intended approach is to show those limits instead of presenting guesses as facts. A recorded attribution link also doesn't prove an ad caused a sale.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Useful Intelligence should explain what it knows—and what it doesn't.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Do you need a separate AI tool?</h2><p>TraceKit Intelligence is intended to work within the platform. Some compatible external AI tools may also access supported TraceKit capabilities through MCP when configured and permitted. This is optional, and specific tools and actions depend on account permissions and production readiness.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>You shouldn't need to understand MCP to benefit from built-in Intelligence.</p></div></section><div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how TraceKit brings business evidence together across the customer journey.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% when 'integrations-mcp-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">INTEGRATIONS & MCP</span><h1>How does TraceKit connect the tools you already use?</h1><p>Your business data lives in different places. Learn what connecting those systems involves and what to check before getting started.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>5 MIN READ</span><span>BEGINNER-FRIENDLY GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / Why are your systems disconnected?</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / What does a connection do?</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Does every listed platform work today?</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / What happens to the original records?</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / What is MCP, in plain English?</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article"><section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Why are your systems disconnected?</h2><p>Advertising platforms know about clicks. Affiliate networks know about attributed conversions. Stores know about orders, and payment providers know about charges and refunds. Each system sees part of the story, so comparing them manually can be difficult.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>The big idea</strong><p>Different tools hold different pieces of the same business story.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What does a connection do?</h2><p>A supported integration can bring available records into TraceKit so your team can investigate how they relate. The exact data depends on the provider and connection: one may support orders, another clicks, and another financial events.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="group" aria-label="Advertising activity then Store orders then Payment outcomes"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Advertising activity</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Store orders</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Payment outcomes</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Does every listed platform work today?</h2><p>Not necessarily. A featured platform on the Integrations page indicates ecosystem relevance, not a promise of a live, fully supported connection. Before onboarding, confirm the exact data types, permissions, sync coverage, and readiness for your account.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A platform logo is not proof that every feature is available.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What happens to the original records?</h2><p>When systems exchange information, it's important to keep their original order numbers, click references, and transaction IDs wherever supported. TraceKit's approach preserves those references so teams can review why records were connected—and see when something can't be matched.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Keep the original evidence, even when information is organized for easier reporting.</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What is MCP, in plain English?</h2><p>MCP is a way for compatible AI assistants to use supported tools from another service. In TraceKit, any external AI access must follow authentication, account permissions, and available product capabilities. You don't need MCP to use TraceKit's built-in Intelligence.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>MCP is an optional connection for compatible AI tools—not another system you must install.</p></div></section><div class="tk-guide-next"><span>CONTINUE LEARNING</span><h2>Explore the connected picture.</h2><p>See how TraceKit brings business evidence together across the customer journey.</p><div><a href="/pages/customer-journeys">Customer Journey Tracking →</a><a href="/pages/integrations">Integrations →</a><a href="/pages/resources">All Guides →</a></div></div></article></div>
{% else %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><h1>Explore TraceKit resources</h1><p>Choose a guide from our learning library.</p><a class="btn" href="/pages/resources">View Resources →</a></div>
